  zero per field.

sink 1460000

The idiomatic C++ wait is a blocking call on a thread the host owns, and
both arms use it. C++ has no carrier-thread notion to pin, so SHAPES.md's
third RPC question is answered trivially here and is a real question only on
a runtime with virtual threads.

`proc-srv CPU` is getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF) minus the server handler's own
CPU, and it is the column to read: it counts EVERY thread in the process,
so the core's tokio workers are in it. `harness-thr CPU` sums
CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID over the threads this harness spawned, which counts
the grpc++ stub's transport (it runs on the calling thread) and MISSES the
core's (it does not) -- it is printed to show the size of that bias, not to
be quoted. Best of 9 rounds, arms alternating which goes first.

The wall-clock column is beside the CPU column and is NOT a throughput
figure: 540 KB per response against a 64 KB default stream window means a
single call in flight spends most of its wall clock waiting for WINDOW_UPDATE.
Both arms' CPU includes the in-process server, which cancels in the ratio.
